module m

// An arm carrying an OR-pattern disables the exhaustiveness check:
// `Violet` has no arm here, and `nova check` says nothing (exit 0).
//
// The same file with the arms written one per name IS refused:
//
//     Red => 1 / Green => 2 / Blue => 3 / Amber => 4
//         -> E_MATCH_NON_EXHAUSTIVE, correct
//     Red => 1 / Green | Blue | Amber => 0
//         -> accepted, WRONG
//     the same, wrapped over two lines
//         -> accepted, WRONG
//     an OR-group with every variant covered
//         -> accepted, correct
//
// Why it matters beyond the miss itself: novac is built on the promise
// that a NEW variant of a sum breaks the build at every place that must
// decide about it. Wherever an OR-group is used to group the "nothing to
// do" cases -- which is the idiomatic way to write a table -- that
// promise silently does not hold.
//
// Found 2026-08-18 by a table of 64 token kinds that was missing two of
// them and compiled clean.
//
// And the program does not even fail loudly: built and run, this file
// prints `0` for `Colour.Violet` -- the uncovered variant yields the zero
// value of the result type and says nothing. A miss that crashes is a
// nuisance; a miss that answers with a plausible number is a defect that
// travels.

type Colour enum Red | Green | Blue | Amber | Violet

fn code(c Colour) -> int {
    match c {
        Red => 1
        Green | Blue | Amber => 0
    }
}

fn main() { println(code(Colour.Violet)) }
